HISTORY AND RELATED FUNCTION PARSERS

This parser (the "Australian parser") is one of three independent
Fortran expression parsers that coexisted in the mid-2000s, each written
from scratch for the same job: evaluating a user-supplied function
string for a large number of variable values, e.g. inside iterative
curve fitting, where parsing once and evaluating many times is
everything.  The other two lines are the "German parser" by Roland
Schmehl and the Brazilian parser by Wilton Pereira da Silva and Ivomar
Brito Soares, written for the LAB Fit curve fitting software.

The cut-down direct evaluator in the simple/ subdirectory of this repo
is the original version.  It is the version the LAB Fit authors found
on the internet, tried, and measured as too slow for non-linear
regression (their 2007 comparative report benchmarks it as the
"Australian parser (Stuart Midgley)").  The faster byte-code compiling
version in the root of this repo -- parse the string once into byte
code with s_createfn, then evaluate it repeatedly with s_evaluatefn --
was developed around the same time, for the same reason.  The ensuing
email correspondence with Ivomar is part of this history; his project
paper cites this code (S. Midgley, Dept. of Physics, University of
Western Australia) and records all three timings.

None of the codes below is derived from this one (and vice versa);
they are independent implementations that have cross-referenced and
benchmarked each other over the years.  The one known direct descendant
of this parser is fortran_script, also by the same author.

Related codes, with links so readers can compare them all:

- fortran_script (companion to this parser, by the same author):
  https://github.com/sdm900/fortran_script
- fparser, the "German parser" by Roland Schmehl (byte-code compiler,
  initf/parsef/evalf API):
  http://fparser.sourceforge.net
  https://sourceforge.net/projects/fparser/
- fortran_function_parser, Jacob Williams' modernization of Schmehl's
  fparser (Fortran 2008, fparser class):
  https://github.com/jacobwilliams/fortran_function_parser
- FortranParser, Jacopo Chevallard's refactoring of Schmehl's fparser:
  https://github.com/jacopo-chevallard/FortranParser
- Fortran Function Parser, the Brazilian parser by Wilton and Ivomar
  (recursive descent, init/evaluate/destroyfunc API), with the 2007
  comparative report benchmarking all three parsers:
  http://www.labfit.net/functionparser.htm
  http://www.labfit.net/report.htm
  https://github.com/jacobwilliams/ffp (unofficial mirror)
- Fortran-Expression-Evaluator, Ivomar's current repository, whose
  README contains the published paper citing this parser:
  https://github.com/ivomarb/Fortran-Expression-Evaluator
- Calmat, a matrix-operations equation parser built on Ivomar's
  interpreter:
  https://github.com/hassaniriad/calmat
- feq-parse, an independent modern equation parser class from
  FluidNumerics:
  https://github.com/FluidNumerics/feq-parse
- M_calculator, John Urban's single-expression evaluator (not a
  byte-code interpreter):
  https://github.com/urbanjost/M_calculator
- A modern multi-parser benchmark page covering several of the above:
  https://davidpfister.github.io/benchmark.f/example_parsers.html

